# Writing a research paper

Tips for drafting abstracts, introductions and the decisions that come before any prose.
Distilled from one paper taken from blank page to submitted abstract, including everything
that was rejected along the way, and from close measurement of two published papers used
as models.

**Useful when** drafting, rewriting, tightening or restructuring a paper or a section of
one, and when an author has rejected a draft and the question is why.

Throughout, **exemplar A** and **exemplar B** are the two measured papers. A is a vision
paper at a top conference; B is an NLP-venue paper in the same subject area. They are cited
only for their measurements.

---

## 0. None of this is a rule

Every tip here comes from a small number of papers, and the two exemplars contradict each
other constantly. One has a contributions list; the other has none. One has no numbers in
its abstract; the other has five. One tapers its paragraph lengths; the other does not.
Where exemplars disagree there is nothing to follow, only a choice to make deliberately.

Three principles below were overturned while being written:

- **"Keep sentences short"** produced prose an author called unprofessional. Exemplar A's
  abstract has a median sentence of 28 words and reads easily.
- **"Never name statistical machinery in an introduction"** is true of both exemplars and
  was wrong for the paper in question, because the statistics were its contribution.
- **"Paragraphs should taper"** was generalised from one paper. The other does not taper.

If a tip makes a specific piece of writing worse, it is wrong for that piece.

---

## 1. Before drafting

**Prose written before the argument is settled gets rewritten, not edited.** Most of the
pain in a long drafting session came from writing sentences while the order of claims was
still moving.

**Writing the questions down first is what makes abstraction a decision.** List the
questions the piece answers, in the order a reader needs them — the big issue, why it is
hard, what we offer, how ours differs, how we know it works, what we found, what follows.
Answer each twice: once in a plain sentence, once in a paragraph. The short answers become
the abstract and the long ones the introduction. When you later ask whether the dataset
size belongs in the abstract, you are choosing between two answers you already have rather
than compressing on the fly.

**Keep that question table where the work happens, and update it when the order changes.**
Order changes are structural. Moving the criticism of prior work from before the method to
after it reshaped four paragraphs in one draft, and the table was the only record of why.

**The venue's page limit is not the budget.** Single-column pages hold roughly half what
double-column pages do, so a 14-page single-column limit and an 8-page double-column limit
are close to the same number of words. Worth also checking whether references count,
whether an extra page exists for statements, which sections must be *removed* for
anonymity, and whether any statement is mandatory on pain of desk rejection.

**If appendices are unlimited, "we cannot fit this" is almost never true.** Every cut
becomes a move, and the only real constraint is that the main text must stand alone.

**Characters are chosen after the answers exist**, because you cannot pick a subject
before you know what the sentences say. See §4.

---

## 2. Register

**The target is a researcher in your broad field who does not work on your specific
problem.** Not a layman, not a specialist. Keep general research vocabulary; drop only
your own machinery.

Words that stay: dataset, subgroup, attribute, counterfactual, synthetic, baseline, false
positive, calibrate, disparity. Words that go to the method section: named estimators,
correction procedures, resampling schemes, variance adjustments, internal scoring
conventions. Both exemplars keep exactly one methodological word in their abstracts, used
as plain English with no definition.

**The failure mode is talking down, and it appears exactly when you are trying to
simplify.** Real rejected lines:

| rejected | replacement | why |
|---|---|---|
| a set of people to test on | a dataset | the audience knows the word |
| we built data with nothing in it | synthetic data generated without the effect | the first says nothing about what was built |
| and be right about it | a significance test for that difference | vague where a term exists |
| the survivors lie in one place | they pair [A] with [B] | "one place" is not a finding |
| harm that falls on someone | harm a model directs at people who… | conversational |

An author's own correction, which is the clearest statement of the target:

> Abstracting things away doesn't mean explaining to a small child. They still use words
> like counterfactual, semantic.

**Simplifying can quietly swap a precise claim for a vague one.** "Fares worse" instead of
"deviates further from the population" reads better and means something different — one
implies an outcome a person receives, the other names what was measured. When simpler
language costs precision, the author should get to choose.

