## 4. Characters and hand-offs

**A character is the grammatical subject a paragraph is about, and a paragraph should have
one.** At most three in a paragraph, and they do not interleave — once the text moves on
from one, it does not come back.

**A character that cannot take a verb is not a character.** Abstract nouns like
"combination" or "variation" fail this; a concrete plural usually replaces them.

**Keep the count small.** Exemplar A's abstract has two characters across seven sentences.
B's has four across nine, in strict sequence.

**A character enters as an object before it becomes a subject.** B's abstract does this
four times in nine sentences:

| # | subject | introduces at the end |
|---|---|---|
| 4 | **we** | → *the tool* |
| 5 | **the tool** | → *its estimates* |
| 6 | **these estimates** | the correlation |
| 7 | **we** | → *the algorithm* |
| 8 | **the algorithm** | the numbers |

Nothing is ever the subject of a sentence on its first appearance.

**Each paragraph can start from what the last one ended on.** B opens ¶2 on "The concept of
intersectionality" immediately after ¶1 ends on "the intersectional nature of these biases"
— the same root word, adjective to noun. A manages this at only two of five junctions and
uses connectives elsewhere, so it is a strong device rather than a requirement.

**When the hand-off word and the character disagree, there are two moves.** Front the
hand-off as a short subordinate clause and let the character take the main verb — *"Because
attributes acting independently already satisfy that criterion, our metric flags only the
subgroups where they do not"* — keeping the fronted clause to six or eight words. Or put
the hand-off in the predicate so the sentence still opens on the character — *"Our metric
is unmoved by attributes whose effects merely add."* Some authors dislike the first.

**Criticism is a minor character.** Both exemplars give it its own main clause only in the
gap paragraph. After their own method appears, every criticism rides inside an "Unlike X"
phrase whose main clause belongs to them. A never names a rival in prose at all — only
bracketed citations — and puts its sharpest charge inside parentheses.

**A consequence worth exploiting:** mention a competitor once, in a subordinate clause, and
you never need a name for it. If the argument needs two mentions, name it descriptively.
"The former", "the older method" and "the standard X" all fail — the first makes readers
count back, the others say nothing about what the thing does.

---

## 5. Paragraph architecture

**An introduction that expands an abstract can keep the abstract's order.** One or two
abstract sentences per paragraph, same characters, same sequence, more room.

**Measured shapes:**

| | exemplar A | exemplar B |
|---|---|---|
| abstract | 187 words, 7 sentences | 184 words, 9 sentences |
| introduction | 817 words, 6 ¶ (14% of body) | 585 words, 5 ¶ (13.6%) |
| ¶ lengths | 93 / 157 / 138 / 181 / 97 / 151 | 223 / 142 / 96 / 79 / 45 |
| contributions list | prose, 3 items, ordinals, **no numbers** | **none** |
| equations or symbols in the intro | **0** | **0** |
| competitors named in prose | **0** | 1, the one they build on |
| citations | all in ¶1–¶2 | all in ¶1–¶2, none after |

B tapers monotonically; A's longest paragraph is its *method* paragraph, fourth of six.

**Citations clustering in the first two paragraphs held for both**, which makes a useful
diagnostic: a later paragraph that needs a citation may be doing work that belongs earlier.

**Two independent claims are often presented as if one follows from the other.** One draft
ran "these groups are hard to find → so a search is needed → a search must rank → ranking
needs a reference". The first three links hold; the fourth does not, because the reference
problem exists whether you search thousands of candidates or hand-pick three. Trying to
state the implication aloud is how you catch it. The repair is to say so: "X is hard for a
mundane reason and a deeper one" costs eight words, and the deeper one — usually the one
your contribution answers — can then take the space it deserves.

**Papers rarely defend what their field treats as standard.** A survey of seven comparable
papers found six stated a methodological choice and moved on, none defended it in an
abstract, and the one paper that defended it in an introduction was defending something
stronger. Where justification appeared, it sat in a discussion or limitations section and
was *ethical* rather than methodological.

**But the design choice underneath it does get explained, in one sentence.** Nobody defends
the standard practice; everybody says why their design holds things fixed or crosses them —
"the only variables that change are the demographics of the subject", "systematically vary
[A] and [B] while holding all other attributes constant".

**A design chosen for identifiability is usually unrealistic as a description of the
world.** Conceding that once, in limitations, costs a sentence and removes a reviewer's
easiest point.

