## 6. Numbers and claims

**A quoted pair of numbers inherits the authority of a controlled comparison.** The most
useful sentence produced in the project. Each number must still be true when the reader
cannot see its denominator.

Exemplar B fails its own test: it reports "fewer steps (2.38 vs 3.15)" where 3.15 is a
*definitional ceiling* rather than a measurement, and "lower bias (0.33 vs 0.52)" where the
comparison ran past its documented design limit. Both read as controlled comparisons.

**The four ways a pair goes wrong:**

| failure | shape | the check |
|---|---|---|
| different procedures | ours significance-tested, theirs an untested count of where a statistic is positive | are both the output of the same kind of operation? |
| different denominators | "200 datasets" beside figures computed on 150 | do all numbers in the passage come from one run? |
| missing denominator | "695 false cells" with no "out of 738" anywhere | can the reader size it? |
| same name, different quantity | "false positive rate" used for a *family-wise* rate — the share of datasets with at least one false flag, not the share of cells wrongly flagged | does the word name what you computed? |

**Scoping a universal costs two words.** "Every published alternative" claims the field;
"every published alternative we tried" claims what you ran. In one session this over-claim
was flagged, removed from a paragraph, and reappeared in the contributions list two turns
later, so it is worth looking for more than once.

**Claims of the form "in all N cases" are the first to break when an experiment is
re-run.** In one re-run with different instruments, several 13-of-13 claims became 12 of 13
and two were dropped. Quantifiers want checking against the current results file, not
against the draft that quoted them.

**Front matter spends its scarce numbers on the problem, not the method.** Both exemplars
do this. B's only motivating figure is a result of its own, over-delivered in an appendix;
its method's headline figures appear in the abstract but are reduced to *adjectives* in the
introduction — "mitigating biases more effectively, producing higher-quality images".

**The unflattering number is usually the one that disappears.** A reports a recall of 0.54
and a correlation of +0.51 in its body; its abstract says "consistent with human
judgements". Worth knowing both when writing and when reading someone else's abstract.

**Conceding a weakness can strengthen a comparison.** "Ours raises a false alarm in 14% of
datasets against a 5% target, where every alternative exceeds 87%" is more persuasive than
quoting only the scenario where ours looks best, because the concession makes the second
clause credible. Picking the scenario where your method looks worst and still wins, and
scoping every figure in the passage to that one scenario, also lets a reader verify all of
them from one table.

