## 7. Abstraction

**Measured against the full papers:**

| | exemplar A | exemplar B |
|---|---|---|
| abstract as share of the paper | **1.4%** | 2.3% |
| numbers in paper → abstract | 700+ → **0** | ~683 → 5 |
| metrics defined → named | 5 → **0** | 16 → **0** |
| models evaluated → named | 10 → **0** | 6 → **0** |
| datasets → named | 3 → **0** | 2–3 → **0** |
| baselines → named | 4+ → **0** | 1 → **0** |
| limitations → mentioned | 4 → **0** | several → **0** |

A's abstract contains no numeral, names none of the ten systems it is built from, cuts one
of its three contributions entirely, and **does not contain the paper's own method name**.

**The selection principle, which two independent measurements converged on:**

> A claim reaches the abstract only if it can be checked against the paper's own
> contributions **without importing any other name.**

> A detail survives only if it marks the difference from prior work, or asserts that
> validation exists at all. Everything contingent is replaceable, and is cut.

Anything whose meaning depends on an outside identity — a model, a dataset, a competitor, a
library — becomes a category or disappears. Anything whose meaning depends on a definition
internal to the paper becomes an everyday near-synonym.

**The moves, with what sits behind each:**

| move | from | to |
|---|---|---|
| sample sizes → one intensifier | 48 images, 100 prompts, 11 conditions, 80 tasks, 5 raters | "**extensive** user studies" |
| multi-stage pipeline → two verbs | five named steps using three systems | "**study and quantify** … using counterfactual reasoning" |
| mechanism → one adverb | chain-of-thought prompting with three verbatim queries | "**automatically** identifies…" |
| named competitor → bare category | four named systems | "unlike **other works** that…" |
| named metric → bare noun | a defined, formula-bearing quantity | "these **estimates**" |
| multi-metric result → one adjective | four quality measures plus a check | "**superior** image quality" |
| hedged case study → capability | "*while this requires manual observation*…" | "**uniquely capable** of…" |
| universal kept, scope dropped | "works for any model, **and we test two**" | "for **any** model" |

**Neither exemplar contains a single inferential term anywhere** — no null, no p-value, no
interval, no correction, not even an agreement coefficient — in abstract, introduction,
method, experiments or appendices. Both validate with descriptive correlations and
sensitivity analyses, and both introductions use a soft verb: "we conduct user studies to
validate our approach with human judgement."

**This is where following exemplars can mislead.** If the statistical framing *is* the
contribution, hiding it hides the paper. Exemplars drawn from a literature that does no
inference will give the wrong register for a venue that does. Structure travels between
venues; register does not.

