## 8. Vocabulary

**When a word carries two senses in one paper, each sense wants its own word.** Two real
collisions: *baseline*, meaning both the reference a metric scores against and the
competing methods you measure against; and *benchmark*, meaning both a dataset you release
and a ground-truth harness for scoring methods. Resolutions: keep "baseline" for the
reference and call competitors "alternatives"; and follow whatever the codebase calls the
two artefacts, so prose and repository agree.

**Listing every noun used more than three times, and asking whether it means the same thing
each time, catches these in one pass.**

**Naming both sides of a contrast is usually better than naming one.** It makes the
contrast do the work and removes every demonstrative at once.

**A term can be defined by its role on first use, at no cost** — "a score of how far a
subgroup departs from a chosen baseline" defines "baseline" in passing, in the sentence
that first needs it.

---

## 9. Figures

**A figure belongs where it does argumentative work, not where the method is described.**
Exemplar A references its only introduction figure in the *gap* paragraph, to show that
prior work's framing is wrong. Its pipeline figure is never referenced in the introduction.

**What the figure shows can override that.** In one case the figure labelled both the
criticised method's reading and the new method's reading in every panel, which meant
introducing the new method *before* the worked example so that a single case carried both
verdicts — the opposite of the exemplar's ordering, for a reason specific to the figure.

**Prose can name quantities in words and leave notation to the picture**, which is part of
how both exemplars keep zero symbols in their introductions.

**Reusing the figure's own numbers in the prose example** means the sentence a reader has
just parsed is the picture they are looking at.

---

## 10. Contributions

**Both forms exist.** Exemplar A has a prose list of three with ordinals; B has no list,
using one paragraph per contribution, each opening by naming its object.

**No contribution carries a number as evidence** in either. Claims are cashed in the
results.

**Items should not overlap.** In one draft, "a metric with a measured error rate" and "a
benchmark that measures any metric's error rate" were the same claim at two scales; merging
them into the second, stronger form fixed it.

**The artefact with no precedent is easy to bury.** In that same draft the most
transferable contribution — a harness other groups could run their own methods through —
sat in a subordinate clause of another item, because it had been treated as an internal
check rather than as a thing.

---

## 11. Naming a method

**Worth doing if the method will be cited as a method** rather than as "the test from that
paper". Conventions nearby: short, pronounceable, often an acronym or portmanteau,
sometimes a plain word with no expansion at all.

**What mattered when choosing:** whether the name encodes the contribution — the reference
it compares against, or the unit it operates on; whether the metaphor matches the headline
result, since a name that recalls the finding is worth more than one describing the
mechanism; whether it is searchable, because common English words are memorable and
unfindable; and whether it over-claims, since a name can read as claiming a new framework
where the honest positioning is an adaptation.

**Forced backronyms read worse than plain words.** Several well-known methods are ordinary
words with no expansion.

---

## 12. When a draft is rejected

**Naming the faults is more useful than producing another draft.** It confirms the
objection was understood and stops the same faults recurring. A diagnosis that worked:

> - an announcement opener *and* a not-X-but-Y, in one sentence
> - sentence 2 is 45 words carrying five ideas, spliced with a semicolon
> - the last sentence is 50 words with a three-item list before the subject arrives
> - median sentence length 28, against 14 for the rest of the draft
> - the character moves five times in four sentences

**Measuring the repair makes it checkable.** "Nine sentences instead of four, median 12
instead of 28, same content" can be verified; "I made it clearer" cannot.

**Prose drifts over a long session** toward stacked clauses, abstract subjects and hedging.
Re-reading the principles periodically beats trusting that they are still being applied.

---

## 13. Traps

**If the paper argues that a way of putting things is wrong, you will put it that way
yourself.** In one draft, a paper arguing against a particular comparison used that exact
comparison in its own definition of the phenomenon — three times in four sentences, and
again later in a sentence whose logic the paper's own thesis refutes. When the thesis is
that a mistake is easy to make, saying so in related work explains the error rather than
merely naming it.

**Restructuring drops the logic along with the words.** One move carried evidence into a
new paragraph and silently left behind the clause that licensed it, so the passage asserted
a comparison with no reason to believe it. Anything moved should bring the sentence that
made it valid.

**Local result files go stale.** In one case they were two weeks older than the documented
headline and three of four aggregates fell outside the current ranges.

**Numbers from two experiments, each correct, produce a false claim** when placed in one
sentence that implies a common denominator.

**Agent-written documents record a reading of the evidence at a moment.** A human decision
supersedes them; noting the risk once and proceeding as decided is the useful response.

---

## 14. Keeping decisions

**Flow comments in the source survive context loss.** A comment above each paragraph naming
which step of the argument it is, what it hands off, and why anything unusual sits there,
lets someone rewriting for language see what has to survive. Marking paragraphs that a
pending result will replace, and saying what the replacement will not affect, keeps a later
swap contained.

**An open-issues list is useful only while it holds open issues.** For each: what is
unresolved, the options, what evidence would settle it. Deleting items when resolved beats
marking them resolved — a list of live decisions is useful, a changelog is not. Where a
resolved decision creates an obligation elsewhere, the obligation stays on the list.

---

## 15. Failures common enough to be worth a second look

Not a procedure — just the things that went wrong more than once.

- A paragraph whose first and last sentences, read in sequence with its neighbours', no
  longer carry the argument.
- A character that changes in the middle of a paragraph.
- A number that cannot be sized without a denominator stated nearby.
- Two numbers in one passage that came from different runs.
- A universal — "every", "all", "none", "first" — that was never scoped.
- An "in all N" that predates the current results file.
- A noun doing duty for two different things.
- A verb stronger than its evidence.
- A sentence over about 35 words, which usually contains two ideas.
- A passage that moved without its justification.
- A defence of something the field finds unremarkable.
- A term first used in a paragraph earlier than the one that introduces it.
